---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import InlineCode from '../../components/InlineCode.astro'
import { url } from '../../lib/url'
import plugins from '../../data/plugins.json'
import schema from '../../data/schema.json'

const { limits } = schema
---

<Base title="How it works" description="What a plugin actually does on your page: the hook it uses in the RUM SDK, the listeners it adds, and the checks every event passes before it is sent." docs>
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A plugin is a few event listeners and a mapping. It hooks into the Datadog Browser SDK you already
    load, listens for what your platform or the page already reports, and turns each event into one
    RUM action. It adds no code to your site.
  </p>

  <h2>1. It plugs into the SDK you already have</h2>
  <p>
    Every plugin is a <code>RumPlugin</code>, the same shape as Datadog's own Shopify plugin. You pass
    it in <code>plugins</code>, and the SDK calls its <code>onInit</code> once, as it starts:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`window.DD_RUM.init({
  applicationId: 'YOUR_APPLICATION_ID',
  clientToken: 'YOUR_CLIENT_TOKEN',
  site: 'datadoghq.com',
  plugins: [window.DD_RUM_PLUGINS['woocommerce']()],
})`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>onInit</code> receives the init options and the SDK's public API. A plugin never initializes
    RUM itself, so your <code>applicationId</code>, sampling and privacy settings stay yours, and it
    calls only public methods: <code>addAction</code>, <code>setUser</code>,
    <code>setGlobalContextProperty</code>, <code>setTrackingConsent</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. It listens where the platform already reports</h2>
  <p>
    What a plugin listens to depends on what the platform exposes. None of them patch your theme or wrap
    your code:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Plugin</th><th>Where its events come from</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {plugins.map((plugin) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href={url(`/plugins/${plugin.name}/`)}>{plugin.title ?? plugin.name}</a></th>
            <td><InlineCode text={plugin.howItWorks?.summary ?? plugin.description} /></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Each plugin page has a step-by-step "How it works", which names every listener it adds and every
    global it reads. A check in CI fails if a plugin touches something its page doesn't explain.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Every event passes the same checks</h2>
  <p>
    A plugin only maps a platform payload onto a schema event. Everything after that lives in the
    shared core, so every plugin behaves the same way, and a new plugin cannot forget a step:
  </p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Declared events only.</strong> The event must belong to a family the plugin declares, and to your <code>events</code> allow-list if you set one.</li>
    <li><strong>Consent.</strong> Until consent is granted, the event is dropped, not queued, so accepting later never backfills it. Identity (<code>setUser</code>) is held the same way.</li>
    <li><strong>The PII filter.</strong> Card fields, addresses, email, phone, passwords and free text are removed wherever they appear in the payload, even when the platform sends them. Email and name reach <code>setUser</code> only if you opt in.</li>
    <li><strong>Size caps.</strong> At most {limits.maxItems} entries in <code>items</code> (sent only with <code>includeItems</code>), and strings cut to {limits.maxStringLength} characters.</li>
    <li><strong>Purchase dedupe.</strong> <code>commerce.purchase</code> is sent once per <code>transaction_id</code> per browser for {limits.dedupeTtlHours} hours, so a reloaded thank-you page does not double-count revenue.</li>
    <li><strong>Your hook.</strong> <code>beforeTrack</code>, if you pass one, can edit or drop the event. Its result is filtered again.</li>
    <li><strong>Sent.</strong> The event goes to <code>addAction</code> with a GA4-style name such as <code>commerce.purchase</code>, carrying session context such as <code>commerce.platform</code> and <code>store.currency</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>4. What arrives in Datadog</h2>
  <p>
    Each action is a RUM event you can search right away, under <code>@type:action</code>, with its
    attributes under <code>@context</code>. Product Analytics picks the actions up with no extra setup.
    Import the <a href={url('/docs/datadog-assets/')}>asset pack</a> and purchases and checkouts also become
    generated metrics, which feed a dashboard and monitors.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    Everything here is measured in the browser, so it undercounts (ad blockers, closed tabs, declined
    consent). Use it next to performance and errors, never instead of your platform's own order
    reporting. <a href={url('/sandbox/')}>Watch a plugin do all of this in the sandbox →</a>
  </p>
</Base>
